From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lef <me@lefteros.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 09:27:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: msgbuf: leave one rx packet ID of slack for
 the refill

The rx packet IDs are shared by the data, ioctl response and event
buffers. max_rxbufpost is capped so that the data buffers get IDs
nr_ctrl + 1 .. NR_RX_PKTIDS - 1, exactly as many IDs as buffers that
may be posted. That is one too few when the ring is refilled.

brcmf_msgbuf_process_rx_complete() calls
brcmf_msgbuf_update_rxbufpost_count() before it frees the completed
buffer's ID with brcmf_msgbuf_get_pktid(). When the posted count drops
to max_rxbufpost - BRCMF_MSGBUF_RXBUFPOST_THRESHOLD, the refill tries
to post 32 buffers while the buffer being completed still holds its
ID, so only 31 IDs are free and the 32nd allocation fails with
"No PKTID available". The refill posts 31 and logs, and the retry for the
last buffer logs again. The posted count then sits one under the cap,
so the next refill, 31 completions later, is short the same way: two
lines for every 31 received buffers.

On a BCM4398 (Pixel 8 Pro) a 300 MB download produced one
"No PKTID available !!" line per 15.7 received packets (13,250 lines
for 208,529 packets); the refill reading predicts one per 15.5.
I saw no throughput problem, which fits: each short refill leaves at
most one buffer unposted.

Cap max_rxbufpost one lower so a full refill always finds an ID.

Signed-off-by: Lef <me@lefteros.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
index b57e6d279..9f152e71e 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
@@ -1886,10 +1886,14 @@ int brcmf_proto_msgbuf_attach(struct brcmf_pub *drvr)
 	msgbuf->max_eventbuf = BRCMF_MSGBUF_MAX_EVENTBUF_POST;
 
 	/* Data, ioctl response and event buffers share the rx packet IDs
-	 * (ID 0 is never used): leave enough for the control buffers.
+	 * (ID 0 is never used): leave enough for the control buffers, plus
+	 * one ID of slack. A completed rx buffer still holds its ID when
+	 * the refill runs (brcmf_msgbuf_process_rx_complete updates the
+	 * post count before it frees the ID), so a refill of N buffers
+	 * needs N free IDs while N - 1 are available.
 	 */
 	msgbuf->max_rxbufpost = min_t(u32, if_msgbuf->max_rxbufpost,
-				      NR_RX_PKTIDS - 1 -
+				      NR_RX_PKTIDS - 2 -
 				      2 * (msgbuf->max_ioctlrespbuf +
 					   msgbuf->max_eventbuf));
 
